Geeklog Plugin Toolkit 0.2.0                                        2026-08-05

The Plugin Toolkit provides tools to assist plugin developers in creating and
maintaining their plugins. The main tool is the Plugin Generator (plgen.php),
which creates a starter plugin structure you can install into Geeklog.

Usage: The Plugin Generator must be called from the command line. You need the
CLI version of PHP.

    cd plugin-toolkit
    php plgen.php

Follow the on-screen instructions. Default values are given in [square brackets]
and can be used by pressing enter. Otherwise, enter your value.

A plugin directory, using the name you entered, will be created in the current
directory. This is a fully usable plugin skeleton: it performs install and
uninstall and includes generic public and admin pages. Filling in the
functionality is your job.

Requirements and recommendations
- Geeklog: plugins generated by this toolkit target Geeklog 2.2.2 by default.
  You can adjust the minimum required Geeklog version when prompted by the
  generator.
- PHP: code generated should be compatible with PHP 7.2+.
- Encoding: language files produced by the toolkit are expected to be saved in
  UTF-8 without a BOM. This allows full support for Unicode (including emoji)
  when using utf8mb4 in databases.

What's new in 0.2.0
- Updated templates and generator for compatibility with Geeklog 2.2.2:
  - autoinstall template: modern $autoinstall[...] structure with a
    pi_gl_version field.
  - SQL templates: CREATE TABLE IF NOT EXISTS, InnoDB engine and
    DEFAULT CHARSET=utf8mb4 by default; primary keys use numeric
    AUTO_INCREMENT fields.
  - Admin templates: example CSRF protection using SEC_createToken()/SEC_checkToken().
  - Language templates: note added to save language files as UTF-8 without BOM.
- plgen.php: toolkit VERSION bumped to 0.2.0, now prompts for the minimum
  Geeklog version (pi_gl_version) and replaces the token in templates.
- All inline code comments in the templates were kept/converted to English.

Notes on generated plugins
- The generator produces a skeleton plugin. You must implement your plugin's
  business logic (functions.inc, public pages, admin actions) and add any
  additional SQL schema required for your data.
- Ensure you use GEeklog APIs and functions appropriate for the version of
  Geeklog you target (2.2.2+). In particular, use SEC_createToken()/SEC_checkToken()
  for CSRF protection in forms and follow current Plugin Developer guidance.

Additional resources
- Plugin Developer Handbook: http://wiki.geeklog.net/index.php/Plugin_Developers_Handbook
- Plugin API: http://wiki.geeklog.net/index.php/Plugin_API

If you want a release or a tagged version created, or if you want me to
generate a sample plugin and run a quick smoke-test (files created, SQL
preview), tell me and I will proceed.

